#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333670 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "addff524-8fb9-5760-a710-1eaf0806f2cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333672} {:answer/id #uuid "bc6936e8-d65a-51c6-a47e-c9913e49b7f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333673} {:answer/id #uuid "c6638664-b046-596c-9880-f021d212a6f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333674} {:answer/id #uuid "8ab2dd6b-9c79-5fe4-9925-637166131c3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333675} {:answer/id #uuid "645c3376-2379-5b97-9d16-5b2e31efb73b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(x)$ has no inflection points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "645c3376-2379-5b97-9d16-5b2e31efb73b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(x)$ has no inflection points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333676} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4caa3e0-51b9-5fe0-9280-4fa651b69b35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e1a1dd3-3388-5f3d-8d7a-a3ff07dd42a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-114419" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/assets/036e249b188b46fe94bd6955fce114cb11dc0f1e1c169fb58d36135cfbc9b797.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $g(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $g(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${g}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${g}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ does not cross the $x$-axis. \n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ has no inflection points."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333677 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf1f1ddb-0270-5e1d-adff-a3b8d9343b08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333679} {:answer/id #uuid "3e5656ac-ac8f-5055-b399-4151121efff7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333680} {:answer/id #uuid "e8041d77-d7eb-58cc-8ea9-6063c5e44c3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333681} {:answer/id #uuid "414ea75b-8727-5abf-83c0-7d6fa0792fcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333682} {:answer/id #uuid "53d9943a-8225-5215-ae4f-3a22c803bbb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333683}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf1f1ddb-0270-5e1d-adff-a3b8d9343b08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333679} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e99b56f-26de-5c42-a6c2-058da69e7299" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f01f8756-8970-5015-bf48-1f08a36f1f26" :question/math-academy-id "q-21044" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=6{x}^{3}+\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=0,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =18{x}^{2}-\\frac{12}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =36x+\\frac{36}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36x+\\frac{36}{{x}^{4}} & =0 \\\\ 36(\\frac{{x}^{5}+1}{{x}^{4}}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{5}+1 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-1.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=-2$ and $x=1$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =36(-2)+\\frac{36}{(-2)^{4}}=-\\frac{279}{4}<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =36(1)+\\frac{36}{(1)^{4}}=72>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-1,$ we conclude that $x=-1$ is indeed a point of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333684 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d249f77e-2330-5878-bf0f-eed7a9569b47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333686} {:answer/id #uuid "0a48152b-7428-5af0-b317-2a40ba1fca1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333687} {:answer/id #uuid "a7789eb0-c545-500f-9db4-c62c1aae5fc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333688} {:answer/id #uuid "d400d042-6b8d-5919-88e8-7968b6797b48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333689} {:answer/id #uuid "d1ef19e4-9c76-5d48-af86-c3384933995e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d249f77e-2330-5878-bf0f-eed7a9569b47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333686} :answer-field/id #uuid "25f144ac-f989-5ca1-a069-599509696ca2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "17c2a431-b9a6-5e59-b365-e7f1bd9e8f80" :question/math-academy-id "q-21045" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-3x+7.$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.  \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={x}^{2}-2x-3 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n2x-2=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=1.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=0$ and $x=2$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =2(0)-2=-2<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =2(2)-2=2>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=1$ is indeed a point of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306064 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation When Some Points Must Be Discarded"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d95b1835-d1ea-5052-bef1-441518bc6883" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306066} {:answer/id #uuid "1c08b0ac-5955-5700-8bf2-c879f2200913" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306067} {:answer/id #uuid "a252231e-cfa1-5bea-8219-390f4b756b2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306068} {:answer/id #uuid "57599abf-0cb2-5d02-9350-ef70e4d42ff2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of inflection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306069} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf3001a-c60d-550a-a637-820b1ba811a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57599abf-0cb2-5d02-9350-ef70e4d42ff2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of inflection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306069} :answer-field/id #uuid "398896fc-e61f-58b1-9a4a-7cc22e7db73b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306065}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "013aa5fa-d294-56b4-a075-76d80070e739" :question/math-academy-id "q-2464" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+4x+4.$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =4x+4 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ has no real solutions, and so there are no points of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333691 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "958f54a6-95cb-5192-8a3a-e12c13b3e6e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333693} {:answer/id #uuid "6ae5b1bb-5e50-5eb2-9662-afa9cb07a12b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333694} {:answer/id #uuid "f73b1650-e5a9-54ac-bfeb-d76db311d3c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1,$ $x=1,$ and $x=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333695} {:answer/id #uuid "c07530c9-601d-5133-90e6-696dc3e202bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333696} {:answer/id #uuid "012d96b4-f5c4-5fdd-ad9b-15a24af95082" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f73b1650-e5a9-54ac-bfeb-d76db311d3c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1,$ $x=1,$ and $x=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333695} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3ae0959-70ec-5fca-85ba-b0492538a831" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7d0fd56-6605-5190-9333-e6bf3c07cfdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-79203" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/assets/2c0a892cd217758e2871f2d3f6f18d7615e79e0f5ad792c751c662e35c69e168.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ and $x=5.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ and $x=5.$"}]}
